bike, but he remembered that it was $4$ digits long, each digit ranged from $0$ to $9$ and that none of the digits were used more than once. How many of these serial numbers are possible?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ digits, so $n=10.$ We want to select $4$ of those digits for the serial number, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different $4$-digit serial numbers is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{\\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =5\\,040.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-1791/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "060e80f0-44bd-5378-9253-999548115dd9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-1791"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-5691"] :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $30$ shows to choose from, so $n=30.$ We want to select $4$ of those shows for the lineup, so we have $r=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different possible lineups is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{30}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{30!}{(30-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{30!}{26!} \\\\  & =\\frac{30\\cdot 29\\cdot 28\\cdot 27\\cdot \\cancel{26!}}{\\cancel{26!}} \\\\  & =30\\cdot 29\\cdot 28\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =657\\,720.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-5558"] :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ athletes, so $n=10.$ We want to select $3$ of those athletes to receive gold, silver, or bronze medals, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to award the medals is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{7!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =720.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-19951/selection/9930e13f32c7645db14afc19c92e7c31070b45701faef710ca7646b43a7debb3" :answer/id #uuid "1e2715cd-94a1-5f02-833c-f631b32ff8a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "990" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19951/selection/e12f73422a456eca8c83cae70999c506825a539631c33c529fb793c051a91c96" :answer/id #uuid "dcd2b0d2-600d-5324-8306-9548b758a37d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "450" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19951/selection/0679661a7e27eeda75d80c6b77fc666524864f141b448c04aed88cb41b526110" :answer/id #uuid "388e8c22-c421-5bbf-a478-463200739fd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,880" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19951/selection/03dfe95972b7d6200b2992c2535cd448742893fb81aca3cb036bb3b39b655a73" :answer/id #uuid "2b622cbd-fe76-569b-abfb-fddc31e4a70b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "900" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19951/selection/c934a131d434e7d3c931048e8a41560c3e62c804b2062f96ba5d40e9fb2d7c3b" :answer/id #uuid "4352abcc-21cf-54de-9fb3-d1d32ee317a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "720" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19951/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "56a29e5b-93e6-5fba-b4ca-da55967d6e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-19951/selection/9930e13f32c7645db14afc19c92e7c31070b45701faef710ca7646b43a7debb3" :answer-field/choices ["q-19951/selection/9930e13f32c7645db14afc19c92e7c31070b45701faef710ca7646b43a7debb3" "q-19951/selection/e12f73422a456eca8c83cae70999c506825a539631c33c529fb793c051a91c96" "q-19951/selection/0679661a7e27eeda75d80c6b77fc666524864f141b448c04aed88cb41b526110" "q-19951/selection/03dfe95972b7d6200b2992c2535cd448742893fb81aca3cb036bb3b39b655a73" "q-19951/selection/c934a131d434e7d3c931048e8a41560c3e62c804b2062f96ba5d40e9fb2d7c3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-19951" :question/id #uuid "5cd3b895-6dde-531c-92f6-31490bca45c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-19951" :question/is-example false :question/problem "There are $11$ people taking an online quiz at the same time. How many ways can people finish in first, second, and third place, assuming there are no ties?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $11$ people, so $n=11.$ We want to select $3$ of those people to come first, second, or third, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to place the people is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{11}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{11!}{(11-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{11!}{8!} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot \\cancel{8!}}{\\cancel{8!}} \\\\  & =11\\cdot 10\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =990.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-19951/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "256d3a08-0ade-51e9-887b-67250d5fae21"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-19951"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122476/selection/a59e10e9ca0b8a69d2b8425f1c9930ee0733ea061a1d6430398562813d750649" :answer/id #uuid "ffa4bf8a-9040-5660-aeb7-52128c689807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122476/selection/39eee5b3d5c62b96b341d749a786eede3dad261dcadf1f1018819ce1e0c69f79" :answer/id #uuid "b2a3ac4a-bb8a-5a36-a844-d093d5be2003" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "151\\,200" :db/ensure [:answ